WEBVTT

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:22.000
Interviewer: Can you describe the physical demands of the heavy truck driver role at your establishment?

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:58.000
Employer: Sure. Our long-haul drivers are behind the wheel for about 90% of their shift — they're seated the entire time they're on the road. But once they reach the depot they're out in the yard climbing loading dock ladders and securing pallets in all weather conditions, rain, ice, you name it. They lift freight up to 75 pounds when strapping down loads.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:14.000
Interviewer: What credentials or training does the position require?

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:38.000
Employer: They need a valid Class A CDL — that's about six to eight weeks of vocational training. No college degree required. We also require a clean MVR and DOT medical clearance before anyone goes on route.

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:52.000
Interviewer: And the pace of work — would you describe it as steady, variable, or high-speed?

00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:10.000
Employer: Variable. Highway miles are steady, but depot turnarounds run on tight automated conveyor schedules. Drivers have a hard 45-minute unload window before the next truck pulls in, so it gets fast-paced at the dock.
